WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:03.819
An admin department at the university
wants to create different reports for

2
00:00:03.819 --> 00:00:06.210
students in the engineering faculty.

3
00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:09.627
The department needs to fill the right
students from the engineering faculty to

4
00:00:09.627 --> 00:00:12.280
retrieve their details
from the student database.

5
00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:14.250
So how can this be done with SQL?

6
00:00:14.250 --> 00:00:18.046
Well, the WHERE clause is
useful in scenarios like this.

7
00:00:18.046 --> 00:00:22.511
In this video, you'll learn how to
explain the purpose of the WHERE clause,

8
00:00:22.511 --> 00:00:25.810
demonstrate how to filter data
using the WHERE clause and

9
00:00:25.810 --> 00:00:29.541
make use of different operators
in the WHERE clause condition.

10
00:00:29.541 --> 00:00:32.189
So what is the WHERE clause?

11
00:00:32.189 --> 00:00:35.713
The WHERE clause is used to filter data,
more specifically,

12
00:00:35.713 --> 00:00:40.950
it is used to filter and extract records
that satisfy specified a condition.

13
00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:43.837
To better understand how
the WHERE clause is used,

14
00:00:43.837 --> 00:00:48.160
it may help to break down its
syntax in a SQL SELECT statement.

15
00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:52.260
The syntax begins with the standard SQL
SELECT statement followed by the columns

16
00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:53.860
you want to query.

17
00:00:53.860 --> 00:00:57.440
Next is the FROM clause
followed by the table_name.

18
00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:01.193
Then you can bring in the WHERE clause,
after the WHERE clause,

19
00:01:01.193 --> 00:01:03.670
you can specify condition.

20
00:01:03.670 --> 00:01:06.960
You may be wondering what
the purpose of the condition is?

21
00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:09.547
Well, the condition makes it
possible to filter out and

22
00:01:09.547 --> 00:01:12.400
fetch the required records from the table.

23
00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:15.199
You can think of the conditions
as filter criteria,

24
00:01:15.199 --> 00:01:18.451
only the records that meet
the condition will be retrieved.

25
00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:23.651
For example, you can use the condition or
filter criteria to check

26
00:01:23.651 --> 00:01:27.761
if the desired column name is equal
to a certain value or operand.

27
00:01:27.761 --> 00:01:31.486
In between the column and
value you can place an operator.

28
00:01:31.486 --> 00:01:35.123
As you've just discovered,
the operand follow the operator.

29
00:01:35.123 --> 00:01:37.141
Let's take a quick look
at it in more detail.

30
00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:41.950
The operand can be either a text value or
a numeric value.

31
00:01:41.950 --> 00:01:45.767
It all depends on the data type
of the table, column or field.

32
00:01:45.767 --> 00:01:51.895
To demonstrate, let's take an example
where student_id equals 01.

33
00:01:51.895 --> 00:01:56.381
In this case the condition is instructed
to filter a numeric value, so

34
00:01:56.381 --> 00:01:58.669
it functions as a filter criteria.

35
00:01:58.669 --> 00:02:03.678
Once you run the SQL SELECT statement,
it retrieves the records as instructed.

36
00:02:03.678 --> 00:02:09.573
Let's take another example, WHERE
first name equals John, a text value.

37
00:02:09.573 --> 00:02:13.218
All text values must be enclosed
in a pair of single quotes.

38
00:02:13.218 --> 00:02:16.623
Once again,
you just run the SQL SELECT statement and

39
00:02:16.623 --> 00:02:18.673
filters the required records.

40
00:02:18.673 --> 00:02:23.434
To specify your filter condition, you can
make use of a wide range of operators.

41
00:02:23.434 --> 00:02:26.201
You've just reviewed an example
of the equals operator and

42
00:02:26.201 --> 00:02:28.760
others you may have encountered
in a previous lesson.

43
00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:31.486
Let's quickly review
these other operators.

44
00:02:31.486 --> 00:02:37.612
SQL comparison operators include equal to,
less than and greater than.

45
00:02:37.612 --> 00:02:43.950
There's also less than or equal to,
greater than or equal to and not equal to.

46
00:02:43.950 --> 00:02:46.492
In addition to these symbols,

47
00:02:46.492 --> 00:02:52.082
the WHERE clause can also use the BETWEEN,
LIKE and AND operators.

48
00:02:52.082 --> 00:02:53.986
With the BETWEEN operator,

49
00:02:53.986 --> 00:02:58.838
you can filter records within a specific
numeric or time and date range.

50
00:02:58.838 --> 00:03:03.407
The LIKE operator is used to specify
a pattern in the WHERE clause filter

51
00:03:03.407 --> 00:03:04.177
criteria.

52
00:03:04.177 --> 00:03:09.498
And the IN operator is used to specify
multiple possible values for a column.

53
00:03:09.498 --> 00:03:14.635
Now, let's explore some examples of
the WHERE clause and SELECT statements.

54
00:03:14.635 --> 00:03:17.565
Recall the scenario of the admin
department that wants to

55
00:03:17.565 --> 00:03:20.627
create reports for
its engineering and science students.

56
00:03:20.627 --> 00:03:24.293
I can use the WHERE clause to filter
out the details of students who are in

57
00:03:24.293 --> 00:03:25.711
the engineering faculty.

58
00:03:26.820 --> 00:03:29.466
In this case I need to
retrieve all details or

59
00:03:29.466 --> 00:03:32.060
all columns from the student table.

60
00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:37.460
So I write, SELECT * FROM student_table.

61
00:03:37.460 --> 00:03:41.610
Next I type WHERE followed
by the filter criteria.

62
00:03:41.610 --> 00:03:45.580
The criteria is written as faculty,
then an equal operator.

63
00:03:45.580 --> 00:03:50.226
Finally, I write engineering and close
in single quotes which are required for

64
00:03:50.226 --> 00:03:52.500
text values or operands.

65
00:03:52.500 --> 00:03:56.099
So I'm instructing my SQL to fetch
only the details of the students who

66
00:03:56.099 --> 00:03:58.890
are attached to the engineering faculty.

67
00:03:58.890 --> 00:04:01.990
Then I run the query,
as per the filter condition,

68
00:04:01.990 --> 00:04:06.679
to retrieved the student records of
the three students in the student_table

69
00:04:06.679 --> 00:04:08.932
listed in the engineering faculty.

70
00:04:08.932 --> 00:04:13.195
Note that I could've used other
operators such as greater than,

71
00:04:13.195 --> 00:04:17.224
less than, less than or equal to,
greater than or equal to and

72
00:04:17.224 --> 00:04:22.354
not equal the same way as the equal to
operator in this WHERE clause condition.

73
00:04:22.354 --> 00:04:27.372
You can use any of these operators
with numeric values or operands.

74
00:04:27.372 --> 00:04:31.086
Now let's review some examples
that use BETWEEN, LIKE and

75
00:04:31.086 --> 00:04:33.916
IN operators in the WHERE
clause condition.

76
00:04:35.249 --> 00:04:39.420
The college has a financial aid program
available to students of a certain age.

77
00:04:39.420 --> 00:04:43.350
The funding department would like
to notify eligible students only.

78
00:04:43.350 --> 00:04:47.312
I can use the BETWEEN operator in
the WHERE clause condition to filter

79
00:04:47.312 --> 00:04:49.439
the records in the student_table.

80
00:04:49.439 --> 00:04:56.062
As before, I type SELECT * FROM
student_table followed by WHERE,

81
00:04:56.062 --> 00:05:01.428
after the WHERE keyword,
I specify the filter column,

82
00:05:01.428 --> 00:05:07.155
a DOB or date of birth,
then I insert the BETWEEN operator.

83
00:05:07.155 --> 00:05:14.591
Lastly, I give the date ranges,
2010-01-01 AND 2010-05-30.

84
00:05:14.591 --> 00:05:19.042
Running this query retrieves the records
of four students whose date of birth falls

85
00:05:19.042 --> 00:05:21.330
in the specified date range.

86
00:05:21.330 --> 00:05:25.378
Note that I could use any numeric
range here, not just dates.

87
00:05:25.378 --> 00:05:29.480
For the next example, let's assume the
admin department requires the details of

88
00:05:29.480 --> 00:05:31.752
the students who are in
the science faculty.

89
00:05:31.752 --> 00:05:36.306
I can do this with the LIKE operator which
can be used when you want to specify

90
00:05:36.306 --> 00:05:39.218
a pattern in the WHERE
clause filter criteria.

91
00:05:39.218 --> 00:05:43.938
Within the SELECT statement and
after the WHERE keyword,

92
00:05:43.938 --> 00:05:47.018
I type faculty, for faculty column,

93
00:05:47.018 --> 00:05:52.137
then the LIKE operator followed
by Sc% within single quotes.

94
00:05:52.137 --> 00:05:56.950
The percentage character in the pattern
is a wild card character that represents

95
00:05:56.950 --> 00:05:59.610
01 or multiple characters.

96
00:05:59.610 --> 00:06:04.133
The underscore sign can also be used
to represent one single character.

97
00:06:04.133 --> 00:06:09.128
In this case my WHERE clause asks my SQL
to search for and filter right values

98
00:06:09.128 --> 00:06:14.699
within the faculty column that start with
Sc followed by any number of characters.

99
00:06:14.699 --> 00:06:20.002
So I run the statement and it filters out
the five student records whose faculty

100
00:06:20.002 --> 00:06:24.834
column has a value of science,
that is, starting with the pattern Sc.

101
00:06:24.834 --> 00:06:29.539
In the final example the admin department
needs to know the details of the students

102
00:06:29.539 --> 00:06:31.940
who are studying in specific locations.

103
00:06:31.940 --> 00:06:36.940
You can use the IN operator in the WHERE
condition to retrieve the relevant student

104
00:06:36.940 --> 00:06:37.602
records.

105
00:06:37.602 --> 00:06:42.677
Remember the IN operator is used to
specify multiple possible values for

106
00:06:42.677 --> 00:06:43.350
column.

107
00:06:43.350 --> 00:06:49.896
Within the SELECT statement and following
the WHERE keyword I type the column_name,

108
00:06:49.896 --> 00:06:55.110
which is country, the IN operator
then an open pair of parentheses.

109
00:06:55.110 --> 00:06:59.521
Within these, I place the values USA and
UK, each in single quotes.

110
00:06:59.521 --> 00:07:04.090
My SELECT query will filter out all
student records that have a value of USA

111
00:07:04.090 --> 00:07:05.870
or UK in the country column.

112
00:07:05.870 --> 00:07:10.627
Running this query returns four records,
two students from the USA and

113
00:07:10.627 --> 00:07:12.379
two students from the UK.

114
00:07:12.379 --> 00:07:16.970
So the IN operator searches for multiple
possible values in the country column and

115
00:07:16.970 --> 00:07:19.300
filters out based on them.

116
00:07:19.300 --> 00:07:22.871
Note that although the examples in this
video looked at the WHERE clause in

117
00:07:22.871 --> 00:07:25.982
the SELECT statement,
it can also be used in other statements,

118
00:07:25.982 --> 00:07:27.980
such as UPDATE and DELETE.

119
00:07:27.980 --> 00:07:32.403
You've now learned what the WHERE clause
is and you should now know how to use

120
00:07:32.403 --> 00:07:36.500
it to filter data as well as how to
use different operators, great work